================================================================================
eSIM BATCH MANAGEMENT — DESIGN PLAN (planning only, no implementation yet)
================================================================================
Scope of this plan (from user request):
  1. Add a checkbox before each COM port row.
  2. Filter the port list and operate eSIM on the *selected* ports (batch).
  3. Support various download source types: QR-code image, text-string code
     (activation codes), sourced from the `esim/` folder.
  4. Design a flow for BATCH download / delete / activate / deactivate.
  5. Hardware reality: each eUICC holds exactly ONE profile (single-profile,
     not multi-profile). The design must respect this.

================================================================================
0. Ground truth gathered
================================================================================
- Activation sources found in `esim/com_port/`:
    - esim_list.txt  -> lines containing `LPA:1$<smdp>$<matchingId>` (sometimes
      with extra tokens/phone numbers on the same line, whitespace separated).
    - 1.jpg, 2.jpg, 3.jpg, 4.png -> QR images encoding an `LPA:1$...` string
      (plus human-readable SM-DP+ address text in the image).
- Canonical activation-code format: `LPA:1$<smdp-domain>$<matchingId>[$<confirmationCode>]`.
  lpac already accepts this via `profile download -a "<LPA string>"` OR the split
  `-s <smdp> -m <matchingId> [-c <conf>]`. Our `DownloadRequest` already has both
  `activation_code` and the split fields.
- HARD CONSTRAINT #1 (machine-wide gate): `EsimService.active: Mutex<Option<String>>`
  allows only ONE port in eSIM mode at a time. => ALL batch operations are
  strictly SEQUENTIAL (enter -> op -> exit -> next port). No parallelism.
- HARD CONSTRAINT #2 (single profile per eUICC): a download onto a port that
  already has a profile must first remove/replace it. Enable/disable/delete can
  resolve "the one profile" automatically (no ICCID picker needed).
- Existing per-port endpoints (all under /api/esim, admin-auth):
  ports, {com}/session/enter, {com}/session/exit, {com}/reset, {com}/chip,
  {com}/profiles, {com}/profiles/download|enable|disable|delete|nickname,
  {com}/notifications*, {com}/provision.
- Existing envelope: every endpoint returns { success, data }. Frontend now
  unwraps this (EsimPage.unwrapPayload).

================================================================================
1. Activation-source model (new)
================================================================================
Introduce a normalized "ActivationCode":
    struct ActivationCode {
        raw_lpa: String,          // "LPA:1$smdp$matchingId[$conf]"
        smdp: String,
        matching_id: String,
        confirmation_code: Option<String>,
        source: String,           // "text:esim_list.txt#L2" | "qr:2.jpg" | "manual"
        label: Option<String>,    // e.g. trailing phone number token, for display
    }

Parsers (new module `src/esim/activation.rs`):
  - parse_lpa_line(&str) -> Vec<ActivationCode>
      Tokenize a text line on whitespace; for each token starting with "LPA:1$",
      split on '$' into (smdp, matchingId, conf?). Non-LPA tokens on the line
      become `label` (e.g. the "0493913181" phone number).
  - parse_text_file(path) -> Vec<ActivationCode>   (one file, many lines)
  - decode_qr_image(bytes) -> Vec<ActivationCode>  (decode QR, then parse_lpa_line)
  - scan_source_dir(dir) -> Vec<ActivationCode>    (walk `esim_source_dir`: parse
      *.txt as text, *.jpg/*.jpeg/*.png as QR; skip unreadable; keep source tag)

QR decode decision: do it in the BACKEND (Rust) so both the server-side folder
and browser uploads share one implementation.
  - New deps: `rqrr` (pure-Rust QR detect/decode) + `image` (load jpg/png).
  - Rejected alt: frontend jsQR — would duplicate parsing and can't read the
    server `esim/` folder. (Could still be added later for pure client convenience.)

================================================================================
2. Config additions (src/config.rs Settings, all optional/back-compat)
================================================================================
- esim_source_dir: Option<String>          (default "./esim/com_port")
- esim_batch_auto_enable: Option<bool>      (default true; DECIDED: auto-enable
                                             immediately after download)
- esim_batch_replace_existing: Option<bool> (default true; DECIDED: auto-replace
                                             an existing profile before download)
- esim_batch_stop_on_error: Option<bool>    (default false: continue to next port)
- lpac_exe already resolves to ./lpac/lpac.exe (CONFIRMED: bundled lpac folder).
Document all in config.toml.example.

================================================================================
3. Single-profile-aware service helpers (new, src/esim/mod.rs)
================================================================================
Because each eUICC has one profile, add helpers that resolve it internally so the
UI never needs an ICCID for the common case:
  - current_profile(com) -> Option<ProfileInfo>   (profile_list -> first/only)
  - enable_current(com)   -> enable the one profile (refresh=true)
  - disable_current(com)  -> disable the one profile
  - delete_current(com)   -> delete the one profile
  - replace_download(com, code, auto_enable):
        list -> if a profile exists: (disable if enabled) + delete -> download ->
        optional enable -> process notifications.
Each helper is still called INSIDE an enter/exit span (see batch runner).

================================================================================
4. Batch job orchestrator (new, backend)  — PRIMARY design
================================================================================
Why backend (not a frontend loop): the sequential gate + enter/exit + lpac steps
are long-running and must survive tab focus changes; a server job is atomic and
auditable.

Model:
  enum BatchOp { Download, Delete, Activate, Deactivate }
  struct BatchItem {
      com_port: String,
      activation: Option<ActivationCode>,   // required for Download only
  }
  struct BatchJob {
      id: String,                 // uuid
      op: BatchOp,
      items: Vec<BatchItem>,
      options: { auto_enable, replace_existing, stop_on_error },
      created_at,
  }
  struct BatchItemResult { com_port, status: pending|running|ok|failed|skipped,
                           message, iccid?, started_at, finished_at }

Runner (`src/esim/batch.rs`):
  - A single global tokio task queue (Mutex<Option<RunningJob>>) — only ONE batch
    job at a time (mirrors the hardware gate). Reject a 2nd job with 409.
  - For each item, sequentially:
        acquire gate via existing enter(com)  (RAII-ish: always exit(com))
        match op:
          Download   -> replace_download(com, code, auto_enable)
          Delete     -> delete_current(com)
          Activate   -> enable_current(com)
          Deactivate -> disable_current(com)
        record BatchItemResult; append to esim_operations audit.
        exit(com); on error: if stop_on_error -> abort remaining as "skipped".
  - Cancellation: a cancel flag checked BETWEEN items (never mid-lpac). Current
    item finishes, remaining -> "skipped(cancelled)".
  - Progress: push events to SSE (reuse SseManager with a new event kind
    "esim_batch") OR a dedicated broadcast channel keyed by job id.

Endpoints (new):
  - GET  /api/esim/sources                 -> scan esim_source_dir; return the
                                              pool of ActivationCode (preview).
  - POST /api/esim/sources/upload          -> multipart: images/txt; returns parsed
                                              ActivationCode[] (no persistence).
  - POST /api/esim/batch                   -> body { op, items[], options }; starts
                                              a job; returns { job_id }.
  - GET  /api/esim/batch/{job_id}          -> current job snapshot (all item results).
  - POST /api/esim/batch/{job_id}/cancel   -> request cancel.
  - GET  /api/esim/batch/{job_id}/events   -> SSE stream of progress (optional;
                                              can also poll the snapshot every 1s).

================================================================================
5. Code<->Port pairing (Download only)  [DECIDED]
================================================================================
Single profile per port => Download needs a 1:1 mapping of activation code to
selected port. UI builds `items[]`:
  - PRIMARY pairing = FILENAME -> PORT (CONFIRMED). The numeric stem of a source
    file maps to the COM port: `2.jpg` -> COM2, `4.png` -> COM4, `10.jpg` -> COM10.
    `esim_list.txt` lines (no numeric stem) fall back to sequential pairing over
    any selected ports still unpaired after filename mapping.
    - scan_source_dir must therefore attach a `port_hint: Option<u32>` derived
      from the numeric filename stem (strip leading zeros; ignore non-numeric
      stems). Multiple codes for the same port -> flag conflict, last-wins unless
      user resolves.
  - Manual override: user can still reassign any code to any port (dropdown/row).
  - Validation: warn if a selected port has no mapped code (excluded); warn if a
    mapped port isn't in the current selection (included only if user opts in);
    warn on duplicate code reuse across ports.

================================================================================
6. Frontend (EsimPage.svelte) changes
================================================================================
Selection + filter:
  - New leading checkbox column; header "select all" respects the active filter.
  - Filter bar: free-text (matches com_port or current SIM/ICCID) + quick toggles
    (eSIM-capable only, has-SIM, in eSIM mode). `filteredPorts` derived state.
  - `selected: Set<com_port>` state; selecting is independent of the single-port
    session panel (which stays for one-off manual work).

Batch toolbar (visible when selected.size > 0):
  - Buttons: Batch Download, Activate, Deactivate, Delete, Clear selection.
  - Guards: disabled while a manual session or another batch job is active.

Batch Download dialog:
  - Source picker: (a) "From server folder" -> GET /sources preview list;
    (b) "Upload files" -> drag/drop images or .txt -> POST /sources/upload;
    (c) "Paste codes" -> textarea of LPA lines.
  - Pairing table: selected ports x assigned code (auto-filled, editable).
  - Options: auto-enable, replace existing profile, stop-on-error.
  - Start -> POST /batch -> open Progress panel.

Progress panel (all batch ops):
  - Per-port rows: status badge (pending/running/ok/failed/skipped) + message +
    resolved ICCID. Live via SSE or 1s poll of the job snapshot.
  - Actions: Cancel (between items), Retry failed (new job with failed items),
    Close when done.

Destructive confirm: Batch Delete / Deactivate require a typed/explicit confirm
listing affected ports.

api.js additions: esimScanSources(), esimUploadSources(files),
esimStartBatch(payload), esimGetBatch(id), esimCancelBatch(id),
esimBatchEvents(id) (EventSource).

i18n: EN + ZH keys for all new labels, statuses, dialogs, warnings.

================================================================================
7. Concurrency, safety, edge cases
================================================================================
- Only one batch job machine-wide (mirrors the single eSIM session). 409 otherwise.
- Manual single-port session and batch are mutually exclusive.
- Always exit()/return-to-Normal per port even on failure (reuse existing exit()
  which clears esim_mode and lets the recheck worker re-read ICCID).
- eSIM-mode ports are already skipped by SMS/recheck workers — batch inherits this.
- Download onto occupied eUICC: only proceed if replace_existing, else fail item.
- Notifications processed after download/enable/delete (GSMA), best-effort.
- Network vs card errors surfaced distinctly in item message.
- Audit every mutating item to esim_operations.

================================================================================
8. Testing
================================================================================
- Unit: parse_lpa_line (single/multi token, conf code, trailing label), QR decode
  of the sample images in esim/com_port/, source-dir scan.
- Unit: batch runner state machine with a mocked EsimService (ok/fail/cancel,
  stop_on_error true/false, skipped propagation).
- Manual hardware runbook: select 2-3 ports, batch download from folder, verify
  sequential enter/exit, per-port ICCID after enable, SMS resumes after exit.

================================================================================
9. Milestones
================================================================================
- M1 UI selection: checkbox column + filter + select-all + selection toolbar,
  wired to EXISTING single-port endpoints via a temporary frontend sequential
  loop (quick, no backend changes) — validates UX.
- M2 Activation sources: activation.rs (text+QR), rqrr/image deps,
  GET /sources + POST /sources/upload, source preview UI.
- M3 Single-profile helpers (current_profile/enable/disable/delete/replace_download).
- M4 Backend batch orchestrator + endpoints + SSE progress; move UI off the
  temporary loop onto real jobs.
- M5 Batch delete/activate/deactivate + cancel + retry-failed + confirms + i18n.
- M6 Tests, config.toml.example docs, polish.

================================================================================
10. Open questions (need user decision before build)
================================================================================
Q1. [DECIDED] Filename implies port: 2.jpg -> COM2. Text lines fall back to
    sequential pairing over remaining selected ports. User-editable override kept.
Q2. [DECIDED] Auto-enable profile immediately after batch download = YES.
Q3. [DECIDED] Existing profile on a port during download = AUTO-REPLACE
    (disable if enabled -> delete -> download -> enable).
Q4. lpac location [DECIDED] = ./lpac/lpac.exe (bundled lpac folder).

Still open (proceeding with proposed defaults unless you object):
Q5. Progress transport: SSE stream vs 1s polling of the job snapshot?
    (Proposed: SSE, reuse SseManager; polling as fallback.)
Q6. Persist batch jobs (new esim_batch_jobs table) vs in-memory job + per-item
    audit only? (Proposed: in-memory + per-item audit for v1.)
Q7. QR decode in backend (rqrr+image) vs frontend (jsQR)?
    (Proposed: backend, so the server-side esim/ folder and uploads share code.)
Q8. If a selected port has NO matching source file/code, skip it silently or
    report it as "skipped (no code)"? (Proposed: report as skipped.)
